# Summary of the Bill (H.R. 1, 119th Congress) — Title X, Committee on the Judiciary

## Subtitle A — Immigration and Law Enforcement Matters, Part I: Immigration Fees

**Sec. 100001. Applicability of the immigration laws.**
This section establishes that the new fees in this subtitle apply to aliens as described in the subtitle. It defines all terms used by reference to section 101 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (8 U.S.C. 1101). References to statutory provisions within the subtitle are to the Immigration and Nationality Act unless otherwise stated.

**Sec. 100002. Asylum fee.**
The Secretary of Homeland Security or the Attorney General must charge a fee to any alien filing an asylum application under section 208, set at the greater of $100 or a rule-set amount for FY2025, with annual inflation adjustments (CPI-U, rounded to the lowest $10). Fee proceeds are split: 50% of fees from applications filed with the Attorney General go to the Executive Office for Immigration Review (EOIR), and 50% of fees filed with DHS go to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS) via the Immigration Examinations Fee Account, with leftovers to the Treasury general fund. The fee may not be waived or reduced.

**Sec. 100003. Employment authorization document fees.**
This section imposes a $550 fee (or higher by rule, with CPI inflation adjustments) for initial employment authorization applications by three groups: asylum applicants under section 208(d)(2), aliens paroled into the U.S., and aliens with Temporary Protected Status (TPS) under section 244(a)(1)(B). For asylum applicants, 25% of fees go to USCIS's Immigration Examinations Fee Account (with at least 50% of that used to detect and prevent immigration benefit fraud) and the rest to the general fund; for parolees and TPS holders, all fees go to the general fund. Employment authorizations for parolees and TPS holders are valid for 1 year or the duration of their status, whichever is shorter. No fee waivers or reductions are permitted for any of the three categories.

**Sec. 100004. Immigration parole fee.**
Any alien paroled into the United States must pay a fee of at least $1,000 (or a higher rule-set amount, adjusted annually for inflation), deposited into the Treasury general fund. Exceptions (case-by-case, at the Secretary's satisfaction) include medical emergencies, parents/guardians of minors with medical emergencies, organ/tissue donors, those attending funerals or visiting imminently dying close family members, adopted children with urgent medical conditions, adjustment-of-status applicants returning from brief travel abroad, aliens paroled from contiguous countries to attend immigration hearings, Cuban and Haitian entrants, and aliens paroled for significant public benefit in law enforcement matters. The fee cannot be waived except for these enumerated exceptions.

**Sec. 100005. Special immigrant juvenile fee.**
The Secretary of Homeland Security must charge a fee of at least $250 (or a higher rule-set amount, with annual CPI adjustments) to any alien, parent, or legal guardian applying for special immigrant juvenile status under section 101(a)(27)(J). All collected fees are deposited into the Treasury general fund. Unlike most other new fees, no explicit no-waiver provision is included in this section.

**Sec. 100006. Temporary protected status fee.**
This section amends section 244(c)(1)(B) of the Immigration and Nationality Act to raise the maximum TPS application fee from $50 to $500. The maximum fee adjusts annually for inflation (CPI-U, rounded to the lowest $10) starting in FY2026. All TPS fees collected are deposited into the Treasury general fund, and the fees may not be waived or reduced.

**Sec. 100007. Visa integrity fee.**
A new "visa integrity fee" of at least $250 (or a higher rule-set amount, adjusted annually for inflation rounded to the nearest dollar) is imposed on every alien issued a nonimmigrant visa, in addition to existing visa fees. The Secretary of Homeland Security may reimburse the fee after the visa expires if the alien complied with all visa conditions (including not accepting unauthorized employment) and either departed the U.S. within 5 days after their authorized period ended or received an extension of status or adjustment to lawful permanent resident status. Unreimbursed fees go to the Treasury general fund, and the fee cannot be waived or reduced.

**Sec. 100008. Form I-94 fee.**
Aliens applying for a Form I-94 Arrival/Departure Record must pay a fee of at least $24 (or a higher rule-set amount, with annual CPI adjustments rounded to the nearest dollar). Of the fees collected, 20% is deposited into the Land Border Inspection Fee Account and made available to U.S. Customs and Border Protection for I-94 processing costs, while the remaining 80% goes to the Treasury general fund. The fee may not be waived or reduced.

**Sec. 100009. Annual asylum fee.**
For each calendar year that an asylum application remains pending, the alien must pay an additional annual fee of at least $100 (or a higher rule-set amount, adjusted annually for inflation). All annual asylum fees are deposited into the Treasury general fund. The fee may not be waived or reduced, meaning a pending asylum applicant could pay this fee repeatedly over multiple years.

**Sec. 100010. Fee relating to renewal and extension of employment authorization for parolees.**
Parolees seeking renewal or extension of employment authorization must pay a fee of at least $275 (or a higher rule-set amount, with CPI adjustments); such authorizations are valid for 1 year or the duration of parole, whichever is shorter. Of the fees collected, 25% is credited to USCIS's Immigration Examinations Fee Account and the remaining 75% goes to the Treasury general fund. The fee may not be waived or reduced.

**Sec. 100011. Fee relating to renewal or extension of employment authorization for asylum applicants.**
Asylum applicants must pay a fee of not less than $275 for each renewal or extension of employment authorization based on their asylum application. The section also specifies when employment authorization terminates: immediately upon denial by an asylum officer (unless referred to an immigration judge), 30 days after denial by an immigration judge (unless timely appealed to the Board of Immigration Appeals), or immediately upon BIA denial of appeal. Of the fees collected, 25% goes to USCIS's Immigration Examinations Fee Account and the rest to the general fund; no waivers are permitted.

**Sec. 100012. Fee relating to renewal and extension of employment authorization for aliens granted temporary protected status.**
TPS recipients seeking renewal or extension of employment authorization must pay a fee of at least $275 (or a higher rule-set amount, with annual CPI adjustments); authorizations are valid for 1 year or the duration of the TPS designation, whichever is shorter. Of the fees collected, 25% is credited to USCIS's Immigration Examinations Fee Account and the remaining 75% goes to the Treasury general fund. The fee may not be waived or reduced.

**Sec. 100013. Fees relating to applications for adjustment of status.**
This section imposes a series of new fees payable to the Attorney General in immigration court proceedings, each with annual CPI adjustments: $1,500 for adjustment of status to lawful permanent resident; $1,050 for waiver of grounds of inadmissibility; $500 for TPS applications in court; $900 for appeals of immigration judge decisions (except bond decisions); $900 for appeals of DHS officer decisions; $1,325 for practitioner disciplinary appeals; $900 for motions to reopen or reconsider (with exceptions for certain in absentia removal motions); $600 for suspension of deportation; $600 for cancellation of removal for lawful permanent residents; and $1,500 for cancellation of removal for nonpermanent residents. For each fee, up to 25% is transferred from the Immigration Examinations Fee Account to EOIR, with the remainder to the Treasury general fund. EOIR is prohibited from using any of these fees for the Legal Orientation Program or any successor program.

**Sec. 100014. Electronic System for Travel Authorization fee.**
This section amends the ESTA fee structure under section 217(h)(3)(B) for Visa Waiver Program travelers, adding a new component of not less than $13 per travel authorization alongside the existing not-less-than-$10 component. The authorization for collecting these fees is extended from October 31, 2028, to October 31, 2034. Both fee components are subject to annual inflation adjustments beginning in FY2026.

**Sec. 100015. Electronic Visa Update System fee.**
Aliens subject to the Electronic Visa Update System (EVUS) must pay a fee of at least $30 (or a higher rule-set amount, with CPI adjustments rounded to the nearest $0.25) at enrollment. A new "CBP Electronic Visa Update System Account" is established in the Treasury general fund, receiving all fee revenue except $5 per payment, which goes to the general fund; the Account funds are appropriated for EVUS-related CBP costs without further appropriation. The fee may not be waived or reduced.

**Sec. 100016. Fee for aliens ordered removed in absentia.**
An alien who was ordered removed in absentia under section 240(b)(5) and is subsequently arrested by U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) must pay a fee of at least $5,000 (or a higher rule-set amount, with annual CPI adjustments) as partial reimbursement for arrest costs. The fee does not apply if the in absentia order was rescinded under section 240(b)(5)(C). Of the fees collected, 50% is credited to ICE's Detention and Removal Office Fee Account for retention and expenditure without further appropriation, and the remaining 50% goes to the Treasury general fund. No waivers or reductions are permitted.

**Sec. 100017. Inadmissible alien apprehension fee.**
Any inadmissible alien apprehended between ports of entry must pay a fee of at least $5,000 (or a higher rule-set amount, with annual CPI adjustments) at the time of apprehension. The section contains two disposition provisions: one crediting 50% of fees to ICE's Detention and Removal Office Fee Account (with the rest to the general fund) and another stating all fees go to the general fund, creating an internal inconsistency. No fee waivers or reductions are authorized.

**Sec. 100018. Amendment to authority to apply for asylum.**
This section amends section 208(d)(3) of the Immigration and Nationality Act, changing "may" to "shall" regarding fees for asylum applications. It removes the prior statutory cap language limiting such fees and replaces it with language clarifying that nothing in the paragraph limits the Attorney General's authority to set additional adjudication and naturalization fees under section 286(m). This effectively removes the previous statutory ceiling on asylum-related fees.

## Part II — Immigration and Law Enforcement Funding

**Sec. 100051. Appropriation for the Department of Homeland Security.**
This section appropriates $2,055,000,000 to the Secretary of Homeland Security for FY2025, available through September 30, 2029, for twelve purposes. These include hiring and training additional Customs and Border Protection agents, transportation for alien departures/removals, DHS employee and State officer assignments under sections 103(a) and 287(g), enhanced screening/vetting and background checks, fingerprint and DNA collection from aliens entering without valid visas, returns of aliens from contiguous territory, State and local participation in enforcement (including anti-gang and anti-smuggling efforts), removal of "specified unaccompanied alien children" (defined as those found at the border, not trafficking victims, and without a credible fear of persecution), expedited removal of criminal aliens, criminal and gang checks (including tattoo examinations) of unaccompanied children aged 12 and older, and information technology investments.

**Sec. 100052. Appropriation for U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement.**
This section appropriates $29,850,000,000 to ICE for FY2025, available through September 30, 2029 — the largest single appropriation in this subtitle. Funds cover hiring and training additional ICE personnel (prioritizing streamlined hiring of retired ICE personnel), performance/retention/signing bonuses (retention bonuses for 2-year commitments, signing bonuses for new hires committing to 5 years, with written service agreements), recruitment and IT infrastructure, transportation for removals, facility upgrades, fleet modernization, family unity detention (allowing aliens charged only with misdemeanor illegal entry under section 275(a) who entered with children under 18 to be detained with their children), expansion of 287(g) agreements with State and local law enforcement, staffing for the Victims of Immigration Crime Engagement Office, and additional attorneys in the Office of the Principal Legal Advisor.

**Sec. 100053. Appropriation for Federal Law Enforcement Training Centers.**
This section appropriates $750,000,000 to the Federal Law Enforcement Training Centers (FLETC) for FY2025, available through September 30, 2029. Not less than $285,000,000 must support training of newly hired Federal law enforcement personnel employed by DHS and State and local law enforcement agencies operating in support of DHS. Not more than $465,000,000 may be used for procurement, construction, maintenance, improvements, and training equipment for FLETC facilities.

**Sec. 100054. Appropriation for the Department of Justice.**
This section appropriates $3,330,000,000 to the Attorney General for FY2025, available through September 30, 2029, for seven purposes. These include hiring immigration judges and staff for EOIR (capped at 800 immigration judges effective November 1, 2028), combating drug trafficking including fentanyl, prosecuting immigration matters (gang crimes, child trafficking, unlawful voting by aliens, Alien Registration Act of 1940 violations, and welfare fraud under title IV of the 1996 Personal Responsibility and Work Opportunity Act), litigation against nonparty injunctive relief suits, increased funding for the Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Grant Program and COPS Office (barred from community violence intervention programs and conditioned on State/local compliance with 8 U.S.C. 1373), maximizing lawsuit settlements payable to the Treasury, and compensating States for incarcerating criminal aliens (felony or 2+ misdemeanor convictions who entered without inspection, faced removal proceedings, or violated nonimmigrant status) — with States ineligible if they restrict cooperation with Federal immigration enforcement.

**Sec. 100055. Bridging Immigration-related Deficits Experienced Nationwide Reimbursement Fund.**
This section establishes the "Bridging Immigration-related Deficits Experienced Nationwide (BIDEN) Reimbursement Fund" within the Department of Justice, appropriating up to $3,500,000,000 for FY2025, available through September 30, 2028. The Attorney General must make grants to eligible States, State agencies, and local governments for locating/apprehending criminally convicted unlawfully present aliens, countering gang activity, investigating and prosecuting crimes committed by aliens and drug/human trafficking, related court operations, temporary detention of aliens, transporting such aliens, and vehicle/logistics support for law enforcement. Grants may reimburse completed, ongoing, or new activities occurring on or after January 20, 2021 (the date of the prior administration's inauguration), and funds must be distributed to more than one State.

**Sec. 100056. Appropriation for the Bureau of Prisons.**
This section appropriates $5,000,000,000 to the Director of the Bureau of Prisons for FY2025, available through September 30, 2029. Not less than $3,000,000,000 must be used for hiring and training new employees — including correctional officers, medical professionals, and facilities/maintenance staff — plus additional salary and benefit funding for the current workforce. Not more than $2,000,000,000 may address maintenance and repairs to Bureau of Prisons facilities.

**Sec. 100057. Appropriation for the United States Secret Service.**
This section appropriates $1,170,000,000 to the Director of the United States Secret Service for FY2025, available through September 30, 2029. Funds may only be used for additional Secret Service resources (personnel, training facilities, programming, and technology) and for performance, retention, and signing bonuses. Retention bonuses go to agents, officers, or analysts committing to 2 additional years of service; signing bonuses go to new hires committing to 5 years of service; both require written service agreements specifying dates, amounts, and termination conditions.

## Subtitle B — Judiciary Matters

**Sec. 100101. Appropriation to the Administrative Office of the United States Courts.**
This section appropriates $1,250,000 for each of fiscal years 2025 through 2028 to the Director of the Administrative Office of the U.S. Courts. The funds support docket analyses and statistical reporting under 28 U.S.C. 604(a)(2), specifically including assessment of the number, frequency, and cost impact of judicial orders granting non-party relief against the Federal Government. This includes examining securities imposed by courts for preliminary injunctions or temporary restraining orders against the Federal Government under Rule 65(c) of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure.

**Sec. 100102. Appropriation to the Federal Judicial Center.**
This section appropriates $1,000,000 for each of fiscal years 2025 through 2028 to the Director of the Federal Judicial Center. The funds must be used for continuing education and training programs for judicial branch personnel under 28 U.S.C. 620(b)(3). The training specifically covers "the absence of constitutional and statutory authority supporting legal claims that seek non-party relief against the Federal Government" and strategies for mitigating the taxpayer cost of such claims.

## Subtitle C — Radiation Exposure Compensation Matters

**Sec. 100201. Extension of fund.**
This section amends section 3(d) of the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act (RECA) to extend the termination date of the RECA Fund to December 31, 2028. It also makes a conforming change replacing "the end of that 2-year period" with "such date." This keeps the compensation fund operational for claims under the expanded program created by the following sections.

**Sec. 100202. Claims relating to atmospheric testing.**
This section expands RECA downwinder compensation for atmospheric nuclear testing, extending the covered period from October 31, 1958, to November 6, 1962, and adding a new eligibility path for individuals physically present in an affected area for at least 1 year between September 24, 1944, and November 6, 1962. Leukemia claimants and specified-disease claimants will each receive $100,000 (up from $50,000/$75,000), with a new condition that no prior payment was made to the individual or their survivor. The definition of "affected area" is expanded to add all of New Mexico (covering Trinity Test downwinders), Utah, Idaho, additional Nevada counties (White Pine, Nye, Lander, Lincoln, Eureka, and part of Clark County), and Arizona counties (Coconino, Yavapai, Navajo, Apache, Gila, and Mohave); for the new 1-year-presence claims, only New Mexico qualifies.

**Sec. 100203. Claims relating to uranium mining.**
This section expands RECA uranium worker eligibility to include core drillers and ore transporters, extends the covered employment period to January 1, 1942, through December 31, 1990, and adds qualifying States: Colorado, New Mexico, Arizona, Wyoming, South Dakota, Washington, Utah, Idaho, North Dakota, Oregon, and Texas. It adds renal cancer and other chronic renal diseases (including nephritis and kidney tubal tissue injury) to the compensable diseases for miners, millers, core drillers, and ore transporters, and covers workers involved in remediation efforts at uranium mines or mills. New special rules allow workers with combined work histories (e.g., part miner, part miller) to qualify — those with at least 1 year in combined positions, or miners whose combined working-level-month exposure meets the Attorney General's threshold. A definition of "core driller" is added for obtaining cylindrical uranium or vanadium rock samples via borehole drilling.

**Sec. 100204. Claims relating to Manhattan Project waste.**
This section creates a new RECA section 5A establishing compensation for individuals exposed to Manhattan Project waste in specified ZIP codes in Missouri (20 ZIP codes in the St. Louis area, e.g., 63031, 63138), Tennessee (14 ZIP codes around Oak Ridge, e.g., 37716, 37830), Alaska (99546, 99547), and Kentucky (42001, 42003, 42086). Living claimants who were physically present in an affected area for at least 2 years after January 1, 1949, and contracted a specified disease receive the greater of $50,000 or their documented out-of-pocket medical expenses (with medical expense claims due by December 31, 2028); surviving spouses of deceased claimants receive $25,000, or surviving children share $25,000 equally if no spouse exists. Specified diseases include leukemia (with exposure after age 20 and onset at least 2 years after first exposure), multiple myeloma, non-Hodgkin lymphoma, and primary cancers of numerous organs (thyroid, breast, esophagus, stomach, pharynx, small intestine, pancreas, bile ducts, gall bladder, salivary gland, urinary bladder, brain, colon, ovary, bone, renal, liver except with cirrhosis/hepatitis B, and lung). Physical presence may be shown via residence, employment, or school attendance, supported by contemporaneous documentation.

**Sec. 100205. Limitations on claims.**
This section amends section 8(a) of RECA to change the claims filing deadline. The previous deadline of "2 years after the date of enactment of the RECA Extension Act of 2022" is replaced with December 31, 2027. This gives claimants under the newly expanded program a fixed cutoff date for submitting compensation claims. [end of text]


